WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:10.761
FAYE MOSS: Hello, and welcome to the latest episode of Superstars in History , the talk show that features interviews with famous people from

2
00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:16.639
the past. Today's guest is a first for our show—she's a real

3
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:24.983
saint! A Catholic nun and missionary, this hardworking woman devoted her life to helping the world's poorest people.

4
00:00:24.984 --> 00:00:48.661
Straight from history, here is one of my favorite superstars . . . Mother Teresa!

5
00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:50.328
MOTHER TERESA: Hello,

6
00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:56.027
Faye. FAYE MOSS: Mother Teresa, it's so great to have you here on

7
00:00:56.028 --> 00:00:59.412
Superstars in History ! MOTHER TERESA: Oh no,

8
00:00:59.413 --> 00:01:06.040
Faye! I'm not a superstar. During my lifetime I just tried to follow a simple

9
00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:13.911
path—reaching out to the poor. FAYE MOSS: You're very humble, Mother Teresa, but we can't wait to

10
00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:20.430
learn more about your extraordinary life. You were born in 1910 in a town in

11
00:01:20.431 --> 00:01:31.496
what is now North Macedonia. MOTHER TERESA: That's right. My Albanian parents named me Agnes Gonxha Bojaxhiu, and I grew up in a house with

12
00:01:31.497 --> 00:01:37.129
a lovely garden and fruit trees. My parents were devout Catholics who were always

13
00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.988
very generous to the poor. I was

14
00:01:39.989 --> 00:01:46.429
especially close to my mother. She taught my brother, sister, and me to love our neighbors and

15
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:51.254
share what we had. We were a happy

16
00:01:51.255 --> 00:01:54.324
family until tragedy struck. FAYE

17
00:01:54.325 --> 00:02:00.705
MOSS: Oh no. What happened? MOTHER TERESA: When I was about eight, my

18
00:02:00.706 --> 00:02:07.987
father died unexpectedly, which left us grief-stricken. My mother was forced to earn money for our family so she started

19
00:02:07.988 --> 00:02:19.992
her own business selling embroidered cloth. It was a hard time, yet Mother still remembered others, often visiting the poor and quietly caring for an elderly neighbor who was

20
00:02:19.993 --> 00:02:28.466
very sick with alcoholism. We also went to church frequently. I joined a youth group, where I heard beautiful

21
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:33.962
stories about missionaries. I especially loved hearing about their work with

22
00:02:33.963 --> 00:02:41.808
children in India. By age 12 I felt a vocation toward the poor. I decided to become a

23
00:02:41.809 --> 00:02:46.675
missionary myself. FAYE MOSS: Six years later you left home to join an

24
00:02:46.676 --> 00:02:53.554
order of nuns. MOTHER TERESA: Yes, I joined the Sisters of Our Lady of Loreto, known for their

25
00:02:53.555 --> 00:03:02.664
missionary work in India. But before traveling to India, I joined the sisters in Ireland to learn English, the language spoken

26
00:03:02.665 --> 00:03:12.605
by the order. In 1928, when I said good-bye to my mother and departed for Ireland, it was the last time I ever

27
00:03:12.606 --> 00:03:14.943
saw her. FAYE

28
00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:23.099
MOSS: That's so sad! MOTHER TERESA: It was sad indeed. Yet doing God's work often

29
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.717
requires families to make sacrifices. After about two months in Ireland,

30
00:03:27.718 --> 00:03:39.017
I set sail for India. As a novice, or nun in training, I worked in Darjeeling, where I taught poor children each day and learned two Indian languages,

31
00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:42.019
Hindi and Bengali. FAYE MOSS: When did

32
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:51.561
you officially become a nun? MOTHER TERESA: I took my vows in 1931. I chose to be called Sister Teresa because

33
00:03:51.562 --> 00:03:56.581
I had always admired Saint Therese, a French nun who'd devoted

34
00:03:56.582 --> 00:04:04.044
herself to praying for missionaries. I decided to spell it the Spanish way—"Teresa"—because another

35
00:04:04.045 --> 00:04:09.573
nun had already taken "Therese." Soon afterward I was sent to the city of Calcutta to teach

36
00:04:09.574 --> 00:04:19.332
at a high school. I worked there for 17 happy years, teaching and later becoming principal. Then one day

37
00:04:19.333 --> 00:04:23.689
the call arrived. FAYE MOSS:

38
00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:33.328
A phone call, Mother Teresa? MOTHER TERESA: Oh no, Faye. It was an inner call. I was traveling on a train when I suddenly

39
00:04:33.329 --> 00:04:43.566
heard a very clear inner voice. It was God commanding me to renounce my life by leaving the convent to work with the poor, living among

40
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:47.582
them in the slums. I got permission from the church

41
00:04:47.583 --> 00:04:57.265
to leave the convent, I traded my nun's habit for a simple white-and-blue sari, the traditional Indian dress, and I moved

42
00:04:57.266 --> 00:05:02.745
to the slums. FAYE MOSS: That was in 1948. What was

43
00:05:02.746 --> 00:05:09.353
Calcutta like back then? MOTHER TERESA: Because of political events, there had been a huge

44
00:05:09.354 --> 00:05:15.158
influx of refugees from Pakistan. Calcutta was packed with shanty towns

45
00:05:15.159 --> 00:05:21.921
made of cardboard and mud. Sewage poured into open drains in the street, and garbage

46
00:05:21.922 --> 00:05:30.843
was left there, rotting. Many people lived in the streets, begging for food. Deadly diseases such as tuberculosis and

47
00:05:30.844 --> 00:05:36.298
leprosy were rampant. FAYE MOSS: That sounds awful. How did you start

48
00:05:36.299 --> 00:05:43.951
helping people on the streets? MOTHER TERESA: At first I didn't know what to do! So I started doing what I knew

49
00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:48.296
best . . . teaching children. But this time my classroom was

50
00:05:48.297 --> 00:05:58.268
in the streets of Calcutta. Since I didn't have anything—not chalk nor books nor paper—I just picked up a stick and scribbled

51
00:05:58.269 --> 00:06:04.185
letters in the dirt. My little school grew quickly, and soon I was given funds to

52
00:06:04.186 --> 00:06:12.686
rent a building. My next step was to open a clinic to treat the sick. Over time other nuns joined me in

53
00:06:12.687 --> 00:06:22.249
my mission. We looked for people who were dying in the streets and carried them to our home, where they could die with peace and

54
00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:30.987
dignity. FAYE MOSS: In 1950 the Vatican recognized you and the other missionaries as an order—the Missionaries

55
00:06:30.988 --> 00:06:39.061
of Charity. MOTHER TERESA: Yes, our mission spread to other countries, and we ran clinics, orphanages,

56
00:06:39.062 --> 00:06:48.713
and soup kitchens. In addition to other vows, we took a vow of poverty, because to know the poor, Faye, you must be

57
00:06:48.714 --> 00:06:55.439
poor yourself. We lived in the slums, sleeping on thin mattresses and owning few

58
00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.397
possessions. Each sister had only three saris, which we washed by

59
00:07:00.398 --> 00:07:10.450
hand. FAYE MOSS: You always treated the poor and sick with dignity, kissing the hands of people with leprosy and hugging patients who were dying

60
00:07:10.451 --> 00:07:18.043
of AIDS. When you walked through the streets of Calcutta, people bent to touch your feet—a sign of deep

61
00:07:18.044 --> 00:07:25.349
respect. Then in 1979, you won the Nobel Peace Prize. What an honor!

62
00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:35.366
MOTHER TERESA: I was not worthy of that honor, Faye. As I often told others, I was nothing, only a pencil in God's

63
00:07:35.367 --> 00:07:43.757
hands. I believe I've said enough about myself for now. Thank you, Faye. I do appreciate your interest in our mission.

64
00:07:43.758 --> 00:07:51.388
FAYE MOSS: Good-bye, Mother Teresa. It was a privilege to meet you! We'll let you return to history

65
00:07:51.389 --> 00:07:55.891
now.

66
00:07:55.892 --> 00:08:01.425
MOTHER TERESA: Good-bye. FAYE MOSS: By the 1990s, doctors were urging

67
00:08:01.426 --> 00:08:09.304
Mother Teresa to retire. But she refused, continuing to work and serve the poor, even with serious health problems such

68
00:08:09.305 --> 00:08:15.146
as heart disease. In 1997 she died of a heart attack

69
00:08:15.147 --> 00:08:26.366
at age 87. Often called a living saint, Mother Teresa was named an official saint—Saint Teresa of Calcutta—by the Catholic Church

70
00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:32.112
in 2016. Today her order, the Missionaries of Charity, still carries on her

71
00:08:32.113 --> 00:08:37.363
mission. I hope my interview with Mother Teresa was interesting and

72
00:08:37.364 --> 00:08:45.836
inspiring! I'll be back soon with another guest from the past.

